WEBVTT

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:28.670
It to Beaver, starring Barbara Billingsley, Hugh Beaumont, Tony Dow, Jerry Mathers as the Beaver.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:41.730
Oh, Wally, relax.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:43.700
You're supposed to be enjoying this.

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:46.410
Gee, Mom, I got enough to think about

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:48.050
without worrying about enjoying that.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:56.580
Well, now that's good.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:58.000
I'm going to put another record.

00:00:58.870 --> 00:00:59.630
Gee, awful, dear.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:02.120
Well, I don't think I want to go to any more dances.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:04.780
Oh, Wally, you're doing fine, isn't he, Bieber?

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:06.190
Well, I don't know.

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:09.600
Or dance with a dancing school with other guys.

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:12.820
Here, let me take this.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:15.840
There. Now, come on, let's try it again.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:22.460
Well, that's pretty good, Wally.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:24.800
Only thing is, you're not home near close enough.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:25.680
Here, let me show you what I mean.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:32.650
Hey, see, isn't that easy?

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:36.250
Yeah, well, it's easy for you, but, well, Mom's not your mother.

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:44.730
Is this with Mom in the olden days?

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:51.530
Yes, Beaver, uh, although in the olden days, somehow they never seem quite like the olden days.

00:01:57.430 --> 00:01:58.470
Oh, hold it, Beave.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:01.460
I want to teach you how to turn around.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:03.880
Oh, well, that might be Eddie Haskell.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:05.490
Well, if he saw me dancing with you,

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:07.050
well, he'd blabber to all the other guys.

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:09.600
Oh, well, excuse me.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:10.960
I didn't know dancing with your mother

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:12.240
was such a serious offense.

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:13.540
Oh, sure, June.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:16.040
That's almost as bad as getting kissed by your father

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:17.100
in public, isn't it, Wally?

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:19.540
Gee, no, Dad. That's worse.

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:22.190
OK, Beef.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:28.700
Hiya, squirt. Where's your brother?

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:39.200
Hi, Eddie. Hi, Wally.

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:41.460
Oh, how do you do, Mrs. Cleaver?

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:43.440
Hello, Mr. Cleaver. Hello, Eddie.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:46.140
Oh, that's a very nice photograph.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.660
My father says a person should listen to good music,

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:51.900
even if he doesn't like it.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:54.420
Well, that was dance music, Eddie.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:57.080
Um, you want to go to my room, Eddie?

00:02:58.720 --> 00:02:59.860
That's all right. Well, me, Wally.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:01.160
Excuse me.

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:03.690
Wally, before you make any plans,

00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:05.850
don't forget you and your father going downtown this afternoon

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:06.570
to buy a new suit.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:10.090
Oh, gee, do we have to go today?

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:11.800
Oh, come on, Wally.

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:13.660
You're the one who wanted the suit for the dance.

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:15.500
Saturday's the only day I have to take you.

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:18.880
Well, I'll go home, Mr. Cleaver, if you feel I'm in the way.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:21.340
Well, as a matter of fact, Eddie.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:22.320
It's all right, Eddie.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:23.300
It's all right, Eddie.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:25.860
Wally and his father aren't going until after lunch.

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:26.820
Come on, Eddie.

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:32.530
Where are you going, Beaver?

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:34.140
Well, I'm going upstairs.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:37.010
I miss it if they start saying any good stuff.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:43.320
Lord, that Eddie.

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:47.080
Do you suppose Wally's that polite when he goes visiting?

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:48.480
I don't know.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.740
But if he is, I hope he's a little more sincere about it.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:52.900
Come on, old folks.

00:03:58.220 --> 00:03:59.970
Wally, I hear you're taking Mary.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.760
Alan Rodgers to the dance next Saturday.

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:03.740
Yeah, well, I might.

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:04.560
What about it?

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:07.470
Uh, nothing.

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:09.790
But I just, uh, heard that, uh...

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:12.000
You heard what?

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:14.800
Well, I heard that, uh...

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:19.820
Hey, Squirt, what are you doing?

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:20.610
Listening.

00:04:21.590 --> 00:04:24.060
Hey, Eddie, we better talk about something else.

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:29.320
Hey, too bad you gotta go shopping with your pop this afternoon.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:29.960
Me and Chuck...

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:33.700
They just got in a couple new racks.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:36.740
Yeah, well, I've got to go down and get a new suit for the dance.

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:39.170
Yeah, well, your father.

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:41.890
When I buy a new suit, I don't go with my father.

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:43.700
You go with your mother, honey?

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:48.940
I'm not a baby. I go by myself.

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:53.370
Gee, I've gone and bought sweaters and junk before by myself,

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:56.030
but, gee, a suit's different.

00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:59.560
Yeah, Mom and Dad always go with us when we buy suits.

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:00.670
Sure.

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:03.540
It's just another way parents have to keep on top of you.

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:06.100
I think they go to help you pick the right stuff.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:07.720
Are you kidding?

00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:10.090
Who needs help when you're in high school?

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:13.850
The least your pop can do is to trust you to buy your own clothes.

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:18.020
Well, I could go meet Chester.

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:20.560
Gee, Eddie, I don't know if I should ask Dad

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:22.200
or not about buying my own clothes.

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:24.800
If you ask him, they always say no.

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:25.940
Just tell him.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:28.500
And then if he says no, you can start whining around.

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:33.090
I'll see you, Wally. Yeah, see you.

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:34.720
Come on, Eddie.

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:44.040
You know, Wally, if I were Eddie's father,

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:45.990
I wouldn't let him buy his own clothes.

00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:47.410
Why not?

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:49.340
Because I don't like Eddie.

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:53.130
Heck, Beaver, when you're guy's father,

00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:54.290
well, you've got to like him.

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:11.190
Beaver, don't eat so fast. Chew your food.

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:13.650
Gee, Dad, this is hamburger.

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:16.000
But you already chewed it in the machine.

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:20.840
Well, he didn't chew the roll, so just slow down, huh?

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:26.120
Warren, what store are you going to take Wally to for his suit?

00:06:26.390 --> 00:06:27.830
Oh, I imagine we'll go to the prep shop again.

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:29.970
Well, he has new black shoes, so why don't you...

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:31.060
get him a dark blue suit.

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:31.460
Dad?

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:32.830
Or maybe a charcoal gray.

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:33.370
Mom?

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:35.140
Do you want another hamburger, Wally?

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:36.160
Uh, no.

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:39.670
Well, gee, I'm in high school now.

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:42.400
Well, how about if I just bought my own suit?

00:06:43.670 --> 00:06:44.490
Bought your own suit?

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:46.520
Well, yeah, you could give me the money,

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:48.680
and I could go down by myself, and I could buy it

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:49.640
just as if you were there.

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:52.900
Oh, Wally, I don't think you're old enough

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:53.880
to buy your own clothes.

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:55.260
Oh, sure I am.

00:06:55.840 --> 00:06:57.560
Wally, name me just one of your friends

00:06:57.560 --> 00:06:58.820
who picks out his own clothes.

00:06:58.920 --> 00:06:59.680
Letty Haskell.

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:03.650
Wally, I agree with your father.

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:06.680
When you buy a suit, you should have someone with you.

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:10.480
Well, sure, uh, well, okay, yeah, I'll take paper.

00:07:10.790 --> 00:07:12.690
Yeah, I can use up some of my judgment.

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:15.870
Wally, we've always picked out your clothes together.

00:07:16.130 --> 00:07:18.290
Now, what's been wrong with any suit you've ever had?

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:24.880
Well, nothing. I guess they're okay, but, well, they're, well, you know, kind of square.

00:07:28.670 --> 00:07:29.070
Square?

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:35.460
Well, Wally, if you don't want the benefit of my experience and advice...

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:36.840
Gee, thanks, Dad.

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:41.660
Just go on down to the prep shop and pick out a suit.

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:44.440
If you think you're old enough to use good judgment,

00:07:45.470 --> 00:07:47.330
let's just see how you make out.

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:54.180
Gee, Dad, it sounds like you're not going to like the suit even before he buys it.

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:57.360
Well, no such thing, Bieber. He can pick out any suit he likes.

00:07:57.460 --> 00:07:58.720
I won't say a word.

00:07:59.130 --> 00:07:59.810
Gee, thanks.

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:01.100
Yeah. Thanks, Mom.

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:01.780
Come on, beef.

00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:09.330
Yeah.

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:13.210
You know something, dear?

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:13.690
What?

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:17.680
You are acting as though you're not going to like it before he's even bought it.

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:20.820
June, I'm completely open-minded.

00:08:22.450 --> 00:08:23.090
Of course you are, dear.

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:26.000
Square.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:29.040
Exactly like the suits I wear.

00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:37.640
Well, that seems to be your size.

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:39.120
How does it feel, son?

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:40.760
Oh, I don't know.

00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:42.000
Well, how does it feel to you, Beef?

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:43.340
Well, I don't know.

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:44.120
You got to...

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:47.920
Well, I don't know if I like it.

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:50.450
Looks kind of like a suit I had once before.

00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:52.650
I never had much fun in that suit.

00:08:52.990 --> 00:08:55.890
Yeah, when you had to wear a dance in school.

00:08:58.100 --> 00:08:59.980
Did your father tell you what kind of...

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:02.820
Oh, no. I'm buying this by myself.

00:09:03.310 --> 00:09:06.530
Yeah. All he said was to calm up about the price.

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:09.520
I think you'd probably like a nice tweed.

00:09:10.370 --> 00:09:11.130
Well, I don't know.

00:09:11.830 --> 00:09:14.660
Well, could we kind of look around by ourselves? Well, you know.

00:09:15.490 --> 00:09:19.090
It's hard making up your mind when somebody's telling you what you ought to like.

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:21.330
Well, you boys go right ahead.

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:25.040
These are your size. Call me when you find something you like.

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:26.260
Gee, thanks, mister.

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:32.260
You know what'd be great, Wally?

00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:33.290
What'd be?

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:35.670
Oh, super jeans.

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:40.380
Hey, get a load of this one.

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:42.900
Boy, this is really weird.

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:46.900
Yeah, make kid's suit so stiff-looking.

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:48.000
Well, I don't know.

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:51.160
Like, he's just to scare you, so while you're in him,

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:52.260
he won't do anything bad.

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:02.830
Hey, boy, look at this one.

00:10:04.270 --> 00:10:05.010
What do you think?

00:10:05.650 --> 00:10:06.250
Well, I don't know.

00:10:06.370 --> 00:10:07.170
What do you think?

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:17.480
Boy, look at all these pockets.

00:10:18.540 --> 00:10:20.020
Hey, it looks pretty sharp from here.

00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:21.680
What does it look like from the back?

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:27.320
It looks pretty sharp from back here, too.

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:29.540
Boy, I never saw a suit like this.

00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:37.910
Hey, they're the same thing.

00:10:38.260 --> 00:10:39.240
We're gonna buy it.

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:41.140
How much does it cost?

00:10:42.460 --> 00:10:44.000
Oh, I forgot about that.

00:10:44.530 --> 00:10:46.990
It'll probably cost twice as much as those weird ones.

00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:51.590
Hey, it's ten dollars less.

00:10:52.300 --> 00:10:52.960
How come?

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:55.340
Because they're crazy, that's why.

00:10:59.330 --> 00:10:59.970
Well, the...

00:11:00.190 --> 00:11:00.690
Found something?

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:02.440
Yeah, this one right here.

00:11:03.670 --> 00:11:03.790
Oh.

00:11:05.050 --> 00:11:06.970
You're quite sure that's what you want?

00:11:07.310 --> 00:11:08.010
Sure, sure.

00:11:08.350 --> 00:11:09.930
It's the best suit in the whole store.

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:12.250
Gee.

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:17.200
Well, it may need a few alterations.

00:11:20.790 --> 00:11:21.190
Hiya.

00:11:21.620 --> 00:11:22.620
Big excitement tonight.

00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:24.880
Wally's upstairs putting on his new suit.

00:11:25.460 --> 00:11:26.560
Oh, finally came, huh?

00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:28.060
She got a look at it?

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:28.500
Uh-uh.

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:29.900
He went down at the store.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:30.880
and herself picked it up.

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:32.340
What's new at the office?

00:11:33.170 --> 00:11:35.590
Oh, Janice is leaving to get married.

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:37.080
Which one is Janice?

00:11:37.890 --> 00:11:39.050
It's a pretty one you don't like.

00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:44.500
Yeah, right here, boy.

00:11:52.940 --> 00:11:54.600
Make a big fuss over it.

00:11:54.620 --> 00:11:56.340
Oh, I'll be positively delighted.

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:00.760
Here it comes.

00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:17.780
That's pretty sharp, huh, Dad?

00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:22.360
Yeah, very sharp.

00:12:22.840 --> 00:12:28.300
Well, I think it's a very nice, Wally.

00:12:31.720 --> 00:12:34.220
Yeah, they tried to sell us a lot of weird ones,

00:12:34.490 --> 00:12:37.990
but we bought this one instead, and we saved you ten whole dollars.

00:12:38.230 --> 00:12:40.650
Hey, look, Mom, it's nice and roomy.

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:44.590
Yes, there is a lot of it, isn't there, Ward?

00:12:45.270 --> 00:12:45.670
Yes.

00:12:47.670 --> 00:12:51.760
Is it my imagination, or is it kind of loud?

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:54.690
Well, what do you mean, loud, Dad?

00:12:56.540 --> 00:12:57.490
Well, bright.

00:12:58.160 --> 00:12:58.780
Well, sure.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:06.400
could spill stuff on it all day and never even notice it. Gee, it didn't look so bright in the

00:13:06.400 --> 00:13:15.770
store. They never do. You mean you don't like it? Well, it's not that your father doesn't like it,

00:13:15.890 --> 00:13:21.050
Wally. It's just that, well, we think that maybe you could have found something a little, uh, a

00:13:21.050 --> 00:13:27.200
little better. Gee whiz, Mom. It had more pockets than any other suit in the store. You just don't

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:29.980
like it because I bought it myself. Now, that's not true.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:32.560
I just don't think you used very good judgment.

00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:36.890
What your father means, Wally, is that, well,

00:13:37.170 --> 00:13:39.150
he's not sure that this is the right suit

00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:40.750
for a nice little dance.

00:13:42.820 --> 00:13:45.140
Well, nobody likes it.

00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:46.720
I like it.

00:13:47.940 --> 00:13:51.400
Well, Wally, now, it isn't that we don't like it.

00:13:51.540 --> 00:13:53.200
It just gives me...

00:13:53.770 --> 00:13:56.660
Wally, really, it's not that we don't like it.

00:13:59.670 --> 00:13:59.890
Oh, Eddie?

00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:00.620
Toohey?

00:14:00.780 --> 00:14:02.060
Good evening, Mrs. Cleaver.

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:02.980
Hello, Mrs. Cleaver.

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:06.020
Wally told us he was getting his new suit today.

00:14:06.290 --> 00:14:07.470
So we came over to see it.

00:14:08.050 --> 00:14:08.530
Oh, well.

00:14:09.030 --> 00:14:09.650
Come on in.

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:11.140
Oh, hi, fellas.

00:14:14.960 --> 00:14:16.580
Yeah, like really, wow.

00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:26.600
Well, you guys like it?

00:14:26.980 --> 00:14:29.440
That's the best suit I ever saw in my whole life.

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:29.840
Me.

00:14:30.970 --> 00:14:33.290
Man is at S-H-A-R-P.

00:14:33.710 --> 00:14:35.470
You picked it out all by yourself?

00:14:35.970 --> 00:14:38.200
All by himself, except I helped him.

00:14:38.740 --> 00:14:41.000
Your parents didn't have anything to do with it?

00:14:42.360 --> 00:14:42.800
Almost.

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:46.930
Look at all those buckets.

00:14:48.030 --> 00:14:48.690
Well, see, Dad?

00:14:49.940 --> 00:14:52.080
The guys think it's real sharp, and so do I.

00:14:52.390 --> 00:14:54.110
I don't think I could ever find a better suit

00:14:54.110 --> 00:14:55.390
if I looked for a million years.

00:14:56.620 --> 00:14:57.500
Look, Wally.

00:14:58.360 --> 00:14:59.640
Couldn't we discuss it later, dear?

00:15:02.110 --> 00:15:03.370
Come on, guys, come on up to my room.

00:15:03.510 --> 00:15:05.430
I'll show you the crazy socks I got to go with it.

00:15:05.450 --> 00:15:07.330
Hey, look at the back of that, isn't that short?

00:15:07.510 --> 00:15:09.050
Too much, too much.

00:15:09.330 --> 00:15:15.260
June, why are you defending that suit?

00:15:15.420 --> 00:15:17.380
That's the most horrible-looking thing I ever saw.

00:15:17.700 --> 00:15:20.120
I'm not defending it, I agree with you.

00:15:20.510 --> 00:15:21.690
But we made a bargain with him.

00:15:21.770 --> 00:15:24.170
Now, you said he could buy a suit and you wouldn't criticize it.

00:15:25.170 --> 00:15:27.830
All right, let him wear that horse blanket to the dance Saturday night.

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:29.900
What are you going to do when our friends see him in it?

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:32.050
I suppose we could move.

00:15:32.210 --> 00:15:33.150
You know, we may have to.

00:15:41.050 --> 00:15:41.770
Dennis Mary.

00:15:42.490 --> 00:15:43.150
John is who?

00:15:44.440 --> 00:15:45.630
The girl from your office.

00:15:46.680 --> 00:15:47.500
Oh, I don't know.

00:15:49.540 --> 00:15:52.380
Probably some character in a plaid suit with six pockets.

00:15:53.180 --> 00:15:54.560
Feeling a little rejected, huh?

00:15:55.430 --> 00:15:57.310
Well, why shouldn't Wally want me to go with him

00:15:57.310 --> 00:15:58.150
to pick out his clothes?

00:15:58.270 --> 00:15:59.970
After all, I've lived a little longer than he

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.860
I've made some mistakes. He could profit by my experience.

00:16:03.580 --> 00:16:05.720
Well, maybe he's entitled to make his own mistakes.

00:16:06.040 --> 00:16:09.180
Do you want him to go through life thinking this is the kind of suit to wear?

00:16:09.810 --> 00:16:13.460
All right, dear, you talk to him, but, oh, Lord, please do it diplomatically.

00:16:14.180 --> 00:16:17.180
Why, he thinks that's the most beautiful suit in the world.

00:16:20.930 --> 00:16:22.070
Hey, hold it tighter, Beef.

00:16:26.650 --> 00:16:28.990
Shine your shoes tonight when the dance is tomorrow night.

00:16:30.350 --> 00:16:31.450
Oh, I just want to be ready.

00:16:32.380 --> 00:16:35.760
It sure is going to be a waste of time if you get there and nobody looks at your feet.

00:16:37.010 --> 00:16:39.670
Well, sure, B, but at a dance, everybody looks at your feet.

00:16:39.810 --> 00:16:45.710
At the dance at school, the only one who looked at my feet was me.

00:16:45.890 --> 00:16:47.790
I wanted to see what they were doing.

00:16:48.570 --> 00:16:52.400
Hi, guys. May I come in?

00:16:52.700 --> 00:16:53.220
Sure, Dad.

00:16:56.310 --> 00:16:59.630
Oh, say, Wally, uh...

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:02.140
I've been thinking about that suit you brought home today,

00:17:02.220 --> 00:17:04.670
and I know I told you I wouldn't say anything about it,

00:17:04.710 --> 00:17:07.090
but I'm afraid we're going to have to have a talk about it,

00:17:07.250 --> 00:17:07.850
man to man.

00:17:08.810 --> 00:17:10.490
You want us to go downstairs, Dad?

00:17:11.260 --> 00:17:12.340
Stick around, Beaver.

00:17:13.080 --> 00:17:14.540
Might profit by this, too.

00:17:17.920 --> 00:17:19.540
You don't like the suit, do you, Dad?

00:17:20.600 --> 00:17:23.940
Well, Wally, it's a question of wearing the right thing

00:17:23.940 --> 00:17:24.720
at the right time.

00:17:25.390 --> 00:17:27.110
It's like once, when I was in college,

00:17:27.170 --> 00:17:29.690
I went to spend a weekend with my roommate at his home.

00:17:30.090 --> 00:17:31.690
Well, the first night I came down to dinner,

00:17:31.900 --> 00:17:33.620
he and his father were wearing jackets.

00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:35.600
I was wearing a turtleneck sweater.

00:17:36.040 --> 00:17:37.360
Why was I embarrassed?

00:17:40.150 --> 00:17:42.790
But gee, Dad, I'm not going to be wearing a turtleneck sweater

00:17:42.790 --> 00:17:43.430
to the dance.

00:17:43.590 --> 00:17:44.770
I'm wearing my new suit.

00:17:45.870 --> 00:17:49.470
I think you understand the point I'm making, Wally.

00:17:53.020 --> 00:17:55.080
That suit is in very bad taste.

00:17:55.450 --> 00:17:56.750
If you wear it to the dance, you're

00:17:56.750 --> 00:17:58.630
going to be embarrassed in front of all your friends.

00:17:59.480 --> 00:17:59.980
Well, gee, no.

00:18:00.090 --> 00:18:00.710
I'm not, Dad.

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:03.220
Eddie and Tui said they were going to get the same kind.

00:18:03.860 --> 00:18:06.040
Ah, I can only like the Three Muskers.

00:18:06.510 --> 00:18:08.450
Beaver, Wally doesn't have to do everything

00:18:08.450 --> 00:18:09.510
Eddie and Tui do.

00:18:09.690 --> 00:18:11.710
If they jumped off a roof, you wouldn't expect him to,

00:18:11.810 --> 00:18:12.030
would you?

00:18:12.430 --> 00:18:13.700
Well, how rough, Dad?

00:18:17.260 --> 00:18:19.650
Wally, all right.

00:18:20.560 --> 00:18:23.800
I guess we just have to learn by bitter experience.

00:18:24.350 --> 00:18:26.270
You go right ahead and wear that suit to the dance.

00:18:26.980 --> 00:18:28.600
I'm not going to forbid it because we made an agreement.

00:18:28.600 --> 00:18:29.980
I told you you could pick out a ..

00:18:30.420 --> 00:18:31.700
and I wouldn't say anything about it.

00:18:32.440 --> 00:18:34.180
But gee, Dad, you've been saying stuff about it

00:18:34.180 --> 00:18:35.300
ever since before supper.

00:18:36.920 --> 00:18:37.700
Now, Wally.

00:18:40.070 --> 00:18:41.110
Oh, how we do it?

00:18:41.710 --> 00:18:43.810
Dad's trying to make me take back the suit I bought.

00:18:44.660 --> 00:18:46.880
Yeah, this suit's got a bad taste.

00:18:48.380 --> 00:18:50.420
Well, I'll tell you, there was only one thing

00:18:50.420 --> 00:18:52.470
that I noticed about that suit, the sleeves.

00:18:53.020 --> 00:18:54.640
Wally, don't you think those sleeves are just

00:18:54.640 --> 00:18:55.640
a little bit too long?

00:18:56.260 --> 00:18:58.460
Gee, everybody's picking on it, so I don't know.

00:18:59.270 --> 00:18:59.970
Picking on it?

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:01.920
It's just that, well, if you're going to wear it,

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:03.300
it seems to me it ought to be just right.

00:19:04.090 --> 00:19:06.720
Well, I guess we could have the sleeves shortened a little.

00:19:07.640 --> 00:19:09.160
I called the store, and they're open tonight.

00:19:10.380 --> 00:19:11.420
Would you drive us down, dear?

00:19:13.400 --> 00:19:14.570
Yeah, I'm so.

00:19:15.760 --> 00:19:18.280
Mom, you're not trying to make me take back the suit, are you?

00:19:18.480 --> 00:19:19.440
Of course I'm not.

00:19:19.800 --> 00:19:20.540
Now, where's the suit?

00:19:20.780 --> 00:19:21.480
It's in the closet.

00:19:25.130 --> 00:19:26.130
That's the guy over there, Mom.

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:28.050
Oh, good evening.

00:19:28.290 --> 00:19:29.310
What may I do for you?

00:19:29.590 --> 00:19:29.970
I'm Mrs.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:33.180
Mrs. Cleaver, I called about having my boy suit altered a little.

00:19:33.440 --> 00:19:34.840
Oh, of course, Mrs. Cleaver.

00:19:48.170 --> 00:19:50.230
Beaver and I'll be around if you need us.

00:19:53.450 --> 00:19:54.290
The neat mirrors.

00:19:54.870 --> 00:19:56.070
It's your back from the front.

00:19:58.660 --> 00:19:59.960
No, just exactly what...

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:00.800
What was it you wanted done?

00:20:01.020 --> 00:20:03.040
Oh, we wanted the sleeve shortened a bit.

00:20:03.420 --> 00:20:05.040
Yeah, you can just chop a hunk off.

00:20:05.600 --> 00:20:07.380
Well, I could do that, but I'm afraid

00:20:07.380 --> 00:20:08.640
that might spoil this suit.

00:20:09.480 --> 00:20:10.460
Well, gee, how come?

00:20:11.240 --> 00:20:12.520
Well, it might spoil the drape.

00:20:13.310 --> 00:20:13.790
What's that?

00:20:14.670 --> 00:20:16.130
Slip your jacket off, I'll show you.

00:20:17.600 --> 00:20:19.360
Dad, there's a million me's in there.

00:20:20.020 --> 00:20:21.320
That's an optical illusion.

00:20:22.810 --> 00:20:25.670
You see, this suit is cut to fit a boy who's

00:20:25.670 --> 00:20:26.730
slighter than most.

00:20:27.300 --> 00:20:27.680
Slider?

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:28.320
That's right.

00:20:28.710 --> 00:20:29.970
That's why the sleeves have this low.

00:20:30.700 --> 00:20:35.140
and there's lots of padding up there to make a boy look big and strong when really he isn't.

00:20:38.260 --> 00:20:40.450
Gee, I just thought a suit was a suit.

00:20:41.010 --> 00:20:42.390
Let me show you something.

00:20:46.420 --> 00:20:48.040
You take a suit like this one here.

00:20:50.840 --> 00:20:52.640
This suit has a lot less padding.

00:20:53.090 --> 00:20:55.670
Most of the huskier fellows seem to prefer this type of suit.

00:20:56.200 --> 00:20:58.780
We saw a lot of these suits at the boys at Mayfield High.

00:21:00.720 --> 00:21:01.080
Really?

00:21:01.580 --> 00:21:02.960
Well, that's a nice suit, all right,

00:21:03.040 --> 00:21:06.250
but, well, I'm afraid Wally has his heart set on this one.

00:21:07.550 --> 00:21:10.110
Um, well, if they can't shorten the sleeves,

00:21:11.190 --> 00:21:12.960
um, well, let me try that one on, OK?

00:21:13.120 --> 00:21:14.100
Just as you say, son.

00:21:15.140 --> 00:21:17.280
Me and Dad were looking at an optical illusion.

00:21:17.620 --> 00:21:19.900
You know, this boy's got quite a build.

00:21:20.300 --> 00:21:22.020
I never noticed it in that other suit.

00:21:26.420 --> 00:21:28.320
They couldn't shorten the sleeves on the other one, Dad.

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:30.720
What do you think of this one?

00:21:31.080 --> 00:21:32.020
Oh, it's up to you, Wally.

00:21:34.190 --> 00:21:36.070
Well, maybe I could try it with the pants.

00:21:36.790 --> 00:21:38.330
Dressing room right in there.

00:21:38.840 --> 00:21:39.180
Thanks.

00:21:40.170 --> 00:21:40.810
Thank you, Wally.

00:21:43.740 --> 00:21:45.540
You know, I think he may take that suit.

00:21:45.930 --> 00:21:46.810
Thank you so much.

00:21:47.160 --> 00:21:48.280
Oh, don't mention it.

00:21:48.460 --> 00:21:50.380
You know, I tried to talk him out of that other suit

00:21:50.380 --> 00:21:50.980
last week.

00:21:51.600 --> 00:21:54.360
And I want to thank you so much for being so diplomatic.

00:21:54.820 --> 00:21:56.440
Oh, this has happened before.

00:21:56.680 --> 00:21:59.900
With other boys who have, shall we say, surprise.

00:22:01.430 --> 00:22:02.890
I'll just see if he needs any help.

00:22:03.160 --> 00:22:03.680
Thank you.

00:22:06.040 --> 00:22:06.980
I have to hand it to you.

00:22:07.420 --> 00:22:08.740
I didn't know what you were doing at first,

00:22:08.860 --> 00:22:11.240
but you're certainly a master of the indirect approach.

00:22:11.810 --> 00:22:12.790
Of course I am, dear.

00:22:14.060 --> 00:22:15.020
How do you think I got you?

00:22:23.620 --> 00:22:26.350
Well, Wally, you really, doesn't he, Warren?

00:22:26.850 --> 00:22:28.770
Oh, yes, in a rugged sort of way.

00:22:29.360 --> 00:22:29.920
Yeah, Dad.

00:22:30.890 --> 00:22:32.850
He even put some of your smelly water on himself.

00:22:39.780 --> 00:22:42.900
Wally, where did you and the other boys at the last minute

00:22:42.900 --> 00:22:44.660
decide not to take dates to the dance?

00:22:45.400 --> 00:22:47.960
Well, Eddie said it's better if we go by ourselves.

00:22:48.430 --> 00:22:50.890
That way, you get to dance with all the other guy's girls,

00:22:51.260 --> 00:22:52.960
then you don't get stuck with them and have to buy

00:22:52.960 --> 00:22:54.000
him stuff on the way home.

00:22:54.260 --> 00:22:57.720
I think that's a very practical approach.

00:22:58.540 --> 00:22:59.600
I think it's horrible.

00:23:05.440 --> 00:23:06.100
Oh, hello, fellas.

00:23:06.240 --> 00:23:08.400
Good evening, Mr. Cleaver, Mrs. Cleaver.

00:23:08.420 --> 00:23:09.100
Hello, Mrs. Cleaver.

00:23:09.120 --> 00:23:09.700
Hello, boys.

00:23:11.470 --> 00:23:12.550
Wally, what happened?

00:23:13.100 --> 00:23:15.080
Yeah, aren't you going to wear your new suit?

00:23:17.000 --> 00:23:19.320
Well, I took it back and got this one instead.

00:23:21.180 --> 00:23:22.160
Oh, what for?

00:23:22.400 --> 00:23:23.480
It was real sharp.

00:23:24.640 --> 00:23:26.500
Well, I like this one better, that's why.

00:23:27.320 --> 00:23:29.860
Gee, we figured you'd knock everybody dead.

00:23:31.120 --> 00:23:32.520
Well, what about you guys?

00:23:32.730 --> 00:23:34.870
You said you were getting the same kind of suit I had.

00:23:36.160 --> 00:23:39.450
Well, I was, but my father made me get this one.

00:23:40.460 --> 00:23:41.360
What about you, Eddie?

00:23:41.930 --> 00:23:43.850
Oh, I would have gotten one just like it.

00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:46.440
But it would have had to come all the way from England.

00:23:50.950 --> 00:23:52.090
You guys wait outside.

00:23:52.930 --> 00:23:53.690
I'll be right out.

00:23:53.870 --> 00:23:54.690
Good night, Mrs. Cleaver.

00:23:54.710 --> 00:23:55.310
Bye, Mrs. Cleaver.

00:23:55.560 --> 00:23:56.080
Good night.

00:23:59.160 --> 00:23:59.680
That's Wally.

00:24:01.050 --> 00:24:03.200
Well, I just wanted to thank you.

00:24:04.240 --> 00:24:05.760
You were right about that other suit.

00:24:06.640 --> 00:24:08.220
I would have looked kind of like a creep in it.

00:24:09.400 --> 00:24:09.740
Uh-huh.

00:24:11.150 --> 00:24:13.130
But gee, Dad, how did you know I would?

00:24:15.580 --> 00:24:18.340
Well, I think you're forgetting about the turtleneck sweater.

00:24:20.640 --> 00:24:22.100
And you were telling me about that.

00:24:22.260 --> 00:24:24.780
Well, I just thought you were yelling at me.

00:24:25.070 --> 00:24:26.950
I didn't know you were trying to tell me anything.

00:24:29.530 --> 00:24:29.930
Come on.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:30.780
You better run along.

00:24:30.960 --> 00:24:31.860
Have a good time, Wally.

00:24:31.960 --> 00:24:32.100
Bye.

00:24:32.140 --> 00:24:32.520
Have fun.

00:24:33.120 --> 00:24:33.480
Bye.

00:24:34.800 --> 00:24:35.920
I'm coming, I'm coming.

00:24:36.120 --> 00:24:41.670
I think Wally's hit on something there.

00:24:42.780 --> 00:24:45.280
We yell at our kids, trying to make an impression on them,

00:24:45.360 --> 00:24:47.600
and all we accomplish is to get them so confused

00:24:47.600 --> 00:24:49.040
they don't understand the word we say.

00:24:51.440 --> 00:24:51.760
Uh-huh.

00:24:51.880 --> 00:24:58.990
I should better.

00:25:01.280 --> 00:25:08.670
Huh? Well?

00:25:30.960 --> 00:25:31.360
Thank you.
